{
  "_readme": "Hand-edit this file to re-skin the app for a different field: rename categories/screens/entities/statuses, reorder or hide screens, regroup them into different categories, or recolor/relabel a status pill. See docs/app_flow_blueprint.md for the full behavioral map this file is a config layer over, and docs/testing_skins_json.md for how the dependency/cascade rules below actually behave when you test an edit. Reload the page after saving — no restart needed. 'id' fields are internal identifiers the code already keys off (nav routing, permission strings, backend status values) and must NOT change; 'label'/'singular'/'plural'/'order'/'category'/'enabled'/'icon'/'color' are the safe fields to edit freely. An optional 'i18nKey' (or 'i18nKeySingular'/'i18nKeyPlural' on an entity) points at an existing translations.json entry so the built-in Arabic translation keeps working for that item unless you also change its wording — leave it out entirely for a brand-new custom label (it will just show literally, in whichever language you wrote it, in every language). A screen's optional 'entities'/'statusGroups' arrays declare which `entities`/`statuses` keys it actually reads — used only to auto-disable that screen (see 'Cross-references / cascading disable' below) if you remove the thing it depends on; they are NOT what wires the reading up in code (adding an id here doesn't make a screen start reading it, removing it doesn't stop a screen from reading it — the JS/TSX still does that). Today only 6 screens actually read `entities`/`statuses` in code — clients/add-client (entity 'client'), clients/dashboard/pending-samples/tech-screen (status group 'visit' or 'client'), and warehouse (entity 'warehouseItem', status groups 'warehouseBill'/'warehouseBatch'/'workOrder') — the rest still show their original hardcoded wording until the same wiring is repeated for them (see docs/app_flow_blueprint.md's customization checklist). Cross-references / cascading disable: a screen is treated as effectively disabled — dropped from the sidebar, and its `showTab()`/deep-link entry point refuses to activate it — if ANY of the following is true: its own `enabled` is `false`; its `category` doesn't match an id under `categories` (or that category itself has `enabled: false`); or any id listed in its `entities`/`statusGroups` no longer has a matching key under this file's `entities`/`statuses`. That last rule is the point of this paragraph: delete (or comment out by removing) an entity or a whole status group, and every screen that declared it as a dependency auto-disables along with it, instead of being left reachable in a broken, unlabeled state. An admin who's logged in sees a one-time on-screen warning naming exactly which screens got auto-disabled this way and why, distinct from a screen you disabled on purpose. Deleting a single status *value* inside a group that still exists (e.g. just `statuses.visit.pending`) does NOT cascade-disable anything — it only makes that one status pill silently fall back to its hardcoded English label/color (a console warning still fires so it isn't invisible).",

